THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_001 adds carriage-closure edges (every attacker of a carrier becomes an attacker of the warrant) and claims the existing fixed point is unchanged with all guarantees surviving verbatim. But closure is not necessarily confluent in the presence of chained carriage: if artifact a1 carries warrant w1 naming target t, and artifact a2 carries warrant w2 naming target a1, then attackers of a2 become attackers of w2, and w2 attacks a1, which can newly expose a1 to self-attacking cycles through w1. The order in which closure edges are added can determine whether a self-attack on a carrier appears before the carrier's own warrant edges are resolved, so the committed edge set is order-dependent unless confluence is proven. The target explicitly admits closure can manufacture self-attack cycles that newly refute previously accepted carriers, which means reinstatement-as-derived and root validity can fail for those carriers.

TARGET:
Maintain carriage-closure as an explicit invariant that expands the edge set the grounded semantics sees without touching the fixpoint algorithm: whenever an artifact carries a warrant naming a target, every attacker of the carrier becomes an attacker of the warrant, enforced at att-construction time (0974daebd1665965, 0378275f89478d38). Because closure is a deterministic, linear-time expansion of the attack relation, the existing fixed point is unchanged and determinism, polynomial cost, reinstatement-as-derived, and root validity all survive verbatim. The criticizable claim: closure can manufacture self-attack cycles that newly refute previously accepted carriers, and the rule must be shown to be confluent — independent of the order in which edges are added — or it introduces an order-dependence into the committed edge set.